# =============================================================================
# Shared reverse proxy environment - /opt/proxy/.env on the VPS
#
#   scp deploy/proxy/.env.example deploy@<vps-ip>:/opt/proxy/.env
#
# Nothing here is a secret, but the file is not committed with real values
# because an address is a deployment fact, not a repository fact.
# =============================================================================

# The site address for JourneyMesh, and the ONLY thing that decides whether
# this VPS serves HTTP or HTTPS. The Caddyfile never changes between the two.
#
#   IP mode, today:
#     JOURNEYMESH_DOMAIN=http://51.79.166.97
#
#   The explicit http:// scheme means plain HTTP and no certificate handling.
#   Let's Encrypt does not issue certificates for a bare IP address, so this
#   is not a degraded HTTPS - it is the only correct configuration until a
#   name exists.
#
#   Domain mode, later:
#     JOURNEYMESH_DOMAIN=journeymesh.example.com
#
#   No scheme means Caddy's default: automatic HTTPS, an automatic redirect
#   from port 80, and renewal roughly 30 days before expiry. Point the A
#   record at this VPS and confirm it resolves BEFORE switching, because a
#   failed challenge counts against a Let's Encrypt rate limit.
#
# Switching is this one line plus:
#   docker compose exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
JOURNEYMESH_DOMAIN=http://203.0.113.10

# Optional. Unused in IP mode, and unused in domain mode unless you uncomment
# the `email` line in the Caddyfile - Caddy issues certificates without it.
# Its only effect is Let's Encrypt expiry warnings.
ACME_EMAIL=

# Uncomment as each application is added, alongside its block in the Caddyfile.
#
# One thing IP mode cannot do: host the second and third application. Caddy
# routes by the Host header, and a bare IP is one host - three sites sharing
# it would collide, and the first block would answer everything. Until there
# are names, this VPS serves one application on port 80. The proxy, the shared
# network and the per-application aliases are all already in place, so adding
# the others later is a variable and a block, not a redesign.
# SAAS2_DOMAIN=saas2.example.com
# SAAS3_DOMAIN=saas3.example.com
